  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nOngoing MCS will continue to cross the region and depart by \nsunrise. Although some gusts to 40 MPH will continue to be\npossible, the decrease of downdraft CAPE and the unfavorable\nlapse rates have pretty much ended the severe threat for\ntonight. Flash flooding threats will continue however, as pockets\nof heavy rain can be expected in the 1.7 to 1.9 inch \nprecipitable water airmass, with training thunderstorms \nproducing localized bands of 2-3 inches of rain in an hour or \ntwo. This threat should end by sunrise, with a lull in activity \nin the subsidence behind the MCS.\n\nAlthough scattered activity may develop this afternoon in the \ndestabilizing and still anomalously moist air mass, the main \nconcern is the next potent MCS that is forecast to begin to \norganize over the Upper Midwest today and track southeast across\nIN/OH and towards the Ohio River. While previous CAMs runs kept\nthis complex mostly off to our south and west, recent HRRR runs\nare starting to trend towards a more northward track, \npotentially impacting a larger portion of eastern Ohio, northern\nWest Virginia, and far southwest Pennsylvania around 00Z \nWednesday. However, HREF/REFS continue to keep the richer CAPE\nto the southwest of CMH, with a gradient just to the southwest\nof the CWA that may represent the previously favored track. The\ncurrent SPC outlook has a Slight (level 2 of 5) Risk for mainly\ndamaging wind gusts south of a PHD/HLG/MGW line. If current \nHRRR trends continue and are supported by additional guidance, \nthis risk area may need to be nudged north with time, with \nperhaps the introduction of an Enhanced (level 3 of 5) Risk to \nin and around the ZZV area at least. If such an expansion \noccurs, the chances of more significant wind gusts (into the \n65-75 MPH range) would increase in the newly upgraded Enhanced \narea.\n\nRegarding future rainfall, HREF/NBM means have generally 1 to\n1.5 inches in the area from around Pittsburgh to the south and\nwest through 12Z Wednesday. HREF maximums suggest 2 to 3 inch\nstripes are possible in the region, with potential for more\nstorm training. The ongoing Flood Watch was extended northward\nanother row of counties, but still remaining south of Pittsburgh\nat this time. Further northward expansion is possible later\ntoday.\n\nIt must continue to be said that this entire forecast period \nwill remain low confidence, with each subsequent storm/threat \nchance tied to the prior. Monitoring the forecast frequently \nmay be imperative. Wednesday and Thursday will bring additional\nchances of severe and flash flooding, though chances of systems\nslowly decrease through time as the development region is \nexpected to drift south into the central Appalachians.",
